{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfxm6iUDEFpcXWS5wD74Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I finished all four steps, and reading the dashboard and rule back confirms the changes.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) uses `timestamp` as its time field. Other fields include `response`/`response.keyword` (the HTTP response code), `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `geo.*`, `machine.os` and `agent`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-ecb40eef Web logs' with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests by `timestamp`.\n   - A bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value (up to 20 codes).\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-ecb40eef 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 server errors arrive within 5 minutes. It counts any response code from \"500\" to \"599\". The response code is stored as text, so this range compares text rather than numbers. That works because every response code has three digits. The rule was enabled when I created it, so I didn't need to turn it on.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to 'Responses by code', and the dashboard now shows that title. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nDashboard: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/s4-ecb40eef-web-logs-a40f12251ea8\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":531,"cache_read_input_tokens":20948,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":531},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da